Obsoletes:      texlive-program-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-program
The main offering is a program environment; a programbox environment is
available for fragments that must not break with the pages.

%package -n texlive-progress
Summary:        Creates an overview of a document's state
Version:        svn19519
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-progress-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-progress-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-progress
Progress is a package which. when compiling TeX and LaTeX documents, generates
a HTML file showing an overview of a document's state (of how finished it is).
The report is sent to file \ProgressReportName, which is by default the
\jobname with the date appended (but is user-modifiable).

%package -n texlive-progressbar
Summary:        Visualize shares of total amounts in the form of a (progress-)bar
Version:        svn33822
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-progressbar-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-progressbar-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(calc.sty)
Requires:       tex(kvoptions.sty)
Requires:       tex(kvsetkeys.sty)
Requires:       tex(tikz.sty)

%description -n texlive-progressbar
This package allows you to easily visualize shares of total amounts in the form
of a bar. So basically you can convert any number between 0 and 1 to a
progressbar using the command \progressbar{<number>}. Also a lot of
customizations are possible, allowing you to create an unique progressbar on
your own. The package uses TikZ to produce its graphics.

%package -n texlive-projlib
Summary:        A collection of facilitative tools
Version:        svn78004
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       texlive-create-theorem

%description -n texlive-projlib
ProjLib is a collection of tools to help you write LaTeX documents. With the
main package ProjLib loaded, you no longer need to set up the theorem-like
environments, nor to manually configure the appropriate multilingual settings.
In addition, a series of auxiliary functionalities are introduced.

%package -n texlive-pronunciation
Summary:        American English Pronunciation
Version:        svn73701
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       tex(ifthen.sty)
Requires:       tex(tipa.sty)
Requires:       tex(xparse.sty)

%description -n texlive-pronunciation
This style file is designed for automatically typing phonetic symbols in
American English. The system supports as many as 21,082 English words all told.
The main packages used in pronunciation.sty are ifthen, xparse and tipa.

%package -n texlive-proofread
Summary:        Commands for inserting annotations
Version:        svn61719
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-proofread-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-proofread-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(marginnote.sty)
Requires:       tex(setspace.sty)
Requires:       tex(soul.sty)
Requires:       tex(tikz.sty)

%description -n texlive-proofread
This package defines a few LaTeX commands that may be useful when you proofread
a LaTeX document. They allow you to easily highlight text and add comments in
the margin. Vim escape sequences are provided for inserting or removing these
LaTeX commands in the source. Options are provided for displaying the document
with extra line spacing, and for displaying it in either corrected or
uncorrected state, both without margin notes. The package is based on code for
a text highlighting command that was published by Antal Spector-Zabusky on
https://tex.stackexchange.com/questions/5959. The main file, proofread.dtx, is
self-extracting, so you can generate the style file by compiling proofread.dtx
with pdfLaTeX. This package is based on the soul package; so if you plan to
highlight non-ASCII characters, you must compile your source with either XeTeX-
